[Chapter "Against ...a6"]
[Line "Calm development"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]

{Black takes b5 away with ...a6. White simply develops.} 1. d4 {Start with the queen's pawn.} 1... d5 2. Nc3 {The Jobava idea: the knight comes to c3 before the c-pawn moves, ready for Nb5 or e4.} 2... Nf6 3. Bf4 {Develop the bishop outside the pawn chain before e3.} 3... a6 {Takes b5 from the knight in advance.} 4. e3 {Solid support for d4.} 4... e6 5. Bd3 {Develop the bishop to its best diagonal, aiming at the kingside.} *
